## 6. Calibration and cold-start

- **Metrics:** Brier score + log loss + a **calibration (reliability) curve** — sklearn docs: proper scoring rules measure calibration *and* discrimination together, so always plot the binned curve (`CalibrationDisplay`) before trusting the number that multiplies margin into "expected contribution".
- **Calibrator choice:** `CalibratedClassifierCV(method="sigmoid")` (Platt) for small samples; isotonic only above ~1000 samples (overfits below that, per sklearn docs). Temperature scaling for multiclass/logit-style outputs.
- **Cold start ladder:** <50 internal samples → priors from cantonal/BKB sector aggregates + the current parametric heuristic (0.55 reference, 0.05/1% slope) as a Bayesian prior on the logistic slope; 50–500 → strongly regularized logistic (or Bayesian logistic with those priors), min/mid/max from coefficient uncertainty; >500 → LightGBM + sigmoid calibration, interval from bootstrap.
- **Always evaluate walk-forward (by tender date)** — bidder markets drift; random k-fold overstates accuracy.
- **Selection-bias guard:** public data contains winners only; never train "P(win | our price)" on award values alone without the price-position normalization, or the model learns bid distributions, not win odds.

